                "<li><strong>Anthropocentrism (Human-Centered Ethics):</strong>\n"
                "<p>Under anthropocentrism, humans stand at the center of the moral universe. Earth and its natural resources exist primarily for human consumption, economic growth, and comfort. Non-human species and ecosystems possess only <em>instrumental importance</em>—meaning they are valuable only to the extent that they serve human interests (such as providing food, timber, pharmaceutical compounds, or recreation). When an anthropocentrist argues for conserving clean water, protecting wetlands, or cutting air pollution, the underlying justification is always to protect human health and preserve resources for the use of future human generations. It is the dominant philosophy embedded in traditional Western legal and economic systems.</p></li>\n"
