                "<tr><td><strong>1980</strong></td><td><strong>The Forest (Conservation) Act</strong></td><td>Massive post-independence deforestation (~1.5 lakh hectares diverted annually for dams and agriculture).</td><td>Halted unilateral state de-reservation; mandated prior Central Government (MoEFCC) approval before diverting forest land for non-forest purposes.</td></tr>\n"
                "<tr><td><strong>1981</strong></td><td><strong>The Air (Prevention & Control of Pollution) Act</strong></td><td>Article 253 legislation implementing the 1972 UN Stockholm Conference resolutions.</td><td>Empowered SPCBs to declare Air Pollution Control Areas, set chimney stack emission limits, and regulate noise pollution (amended 1987).</td></tr>\n"
                "<tr><td><strong>1986</strong></td><td><strong>The Environment (Protection) Act</strong></td><td><strong>Bhopal Gas Tragedy (December 1984)</strong> exposed fatal regulatory voids and fragmented enforcement.</td><td>Overarching 'Umbrella Act' empowering Central Government to fix nationwide standards, manage hazardous wastes, mandate EIA clearance, and issue closure notices.</td></tr>\n"
                "</tbody>\n"
                "</table>\n"
                "<h4>Detailed Historical Progression Across Four Decades</h4>\n"
                "<ol>\n"
                "<li><strong>1972 — Wildlife Protection:</strong> Prior to 1972, forest and wildlife administration fell exclusively under state lists, leading to uneven conservation and widespread poaching. In 1972, Parliament enacted the Wildlife (Protection) Act, creating a uniform national framework establishing protected areas, wildlife advisory boards, and stringent criminal schedules that outlawed commercial hunting nationwide.</li>\n"
                "<li><strong>1974 — Water Pollution & The Birth of Pollution Control Boards:</strong> As industrial manufacturing expanded, untreated factory effluents poisoned major river basins. The Water Act of 1974 established India's first dedicated environmental enforcement agencies: the Central Board for the Prevention and Control of Water Pollution (now CPCB) and State Boards (SPCBs), creating the mandatory <em>Consent to Establish (CTE)</em> and <em>Consent to Operate (CTO)</em> regulatory regime.</li>\n"
                "<li><strong>1980 — Forest Conservation & Central Clearance:</strong> Between 1951 and 1980, India lost over 4.3 million hectares of primary forest to agricultural expansion, urban development, and river valley projects. The Forest (Conservation) Act of 1980 stripped state governments of the unilateral power to clear forests, decreeing that no reserved forest can be de-reserved or diverted for non-forest use without prior concurrence from the Central Government.</li>\n"
                "<li><strong>1981 — Air Pollution & The Stockholm Legacy:</strong> To honor India's international pledges at the 1972 Stockholm Conference, Parliament passed the Air Act of 1981 under Article 253. The Act gave SPCBs statutory authority to regulate industrial smokestacks, inspect boilers, and enforce ambient air standards. In 1987, the Act was amended to formally designate noise as an air pollutant.</li>\n"
                "<li><strong>1986 — The Umbrella Environment Protection Act (Post-Bhopal):</strong> On the night of 2–3 December 1984, the catastrophic release of methyl isocyanate (MIC) gas from the Union Carbide pesticide factory in Bhopal killed thousands and injured hundreds of thousands. The disaster revealed glaring gaps in existing sectoral laws, which lacked mechanisms for hazardous chemical regulation, immediate plant shutdowns, or rapid emergency response. Parliament enacted the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 as an 'Umbrella Act' to coordinate all state and central environmental authorities, set national standards, and serve as the parent law for Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA), Coastal Regulation Zone (CRZ), and Hazardous Waste rules.</li>\n"
                "</ol>\n"
                "<h4>Important Additional Chronological Milestones Tested in Exam Variants</h4>\n"
